## 2. Reusable flow templates (base classes)

Each common business pattern becomes an abstract class. Handlers pick the one they need and fill in the blanks.

### 2.1 The Redis-lookup flow (what we built so far)

```java
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public abstract class NationalCodeLookupHandler<T> implements TopicHandler<T> {

    protected final NationalCodeLookup lookup;

    protected abstract String extractNationalCode(T payload);
    protected abstract Mono<Void> onMatchedEntry(T payload, String nationalCode, RedisEntry entry, MessageContext ctx);

    protected Mono<Void> onNoMatch(T payload, String nationalCode, MessageContext ctx) { return Mono.empty(); }
    protected boolean accepts(RedisEntry entry) { return true; }
    protected int entryConcurrency() { return 8; }
    protected FailurePolicy failurePolicy() { return FailurePolicy.TRY_ALL_THEN_FAIL; }

    /** Override for a batched call instead of per-entry fan-out. */
    protected Mono<Void> onMatch(T payload, String nc, List<RedisEntry> entries, MessageContext ctx) {
        return EntryFanOut.run(entries.stream().filter(this::accepts).toList(),
                               e -> onMatchedEntry(payload, nc, e, ctx),
                               entryConcurrency(), failurePolicy());
    }

    @Override
    public final Mono<Void> handle(T payload, MessageContext ctx) {
        String nc = extractNationalCode(payload);
        if (nc == null || nc.isBlank()) return Mono.error(new InvalidMessageException("nationalCode missing"));

        return lookup.findAll(nc)
                .collectList()
                .flatMap(entries -> entries.isEmpty()
                        ? onNoMatch(payload, nc, ctx)
                        : onMatch(payload, nc, entries, ctx));
    }
}
```

### 2.2 A "call an API directly" flow – no Redis at all

```java
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public abstract class DirectApiHandler<T, R> implements TopicHandler<T> {

    protected final ResilientApiClient api;

    protected abstract String apiName();                 // resilience4j instance name
    protected abstract String uri(T payload);
    protected abstract R toRequest(T payload, MessageContext ctx);
    protected boolean shouldCall(T payload) { return true; }   // cheap in-memory filter

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> handle(T payload, MessageContext ctx) {
        if (!shouldCall(payload)) return Mono.empty();
        return api.post(apiName(), uri(payload), toRequest(payload, ctx),
                        Map.of("Idempotency-Key", ctx.idempotencyKey()));
    }
}
```

### 2.3 A "write to Redis" flow – the topic that *populates* the cache others read

```java
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public abstract class RedisWriterHandler<T> implements TopicHandler<T> {

    protected final ReactiveStringRedisTemplate redis;

    protected abstract String key(T payload);
    protected abstract String value(T payload);
    protected Duration ttl() { return Duration.ofDays(30); }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> handle(T payload, MessageContext ctx) {
        return redis.opsForValue().set(key(payload), value(payload), ttl()).then();
    }
}
```

### 2.4 A "route to another topic" flow

```java
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public abstract class ForwardingHandler<T> implements TopicHandler<T> {

    protected final KafkaSender<String, byte[]> sender;
    protected final ObjectMapper mapper;

    protected abstract String targetTopic(T payload);
    protected Object transform(T payload) { return payload; }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> handle(T payload, MessageContext ctx) {
        return Mono.fromCallable(() -> mapper.writeValueAsBytes(transform(payload)))
            .map(bytes -> SenderRecord.create(new ProducerRecord<>(targetTopic(payload), ctx.key(), bytes), null))
            .flatMap(r -> sender.send(Mono.just(r)).then());
    }
}
```

You'll add more templates as new *patterns* show up — but that's rare; most new topics fit an existing template.

