## 4. Optional: composable steps for mix-and-match flows

If flows start to overlap heavily ("lookup Redis **and then** forward **and then** call API"), add a tiny step abstraction instead of more base classes:

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Step<T> {
    Mono<T> apply(T payload, MessageContext ctx);       // returns (possibly enriched) payload

    default Step<T> then(Step<T> next) {
        return (p, ctx) -> apply(p, ctx).flatMap(out -> next.apply(out, ctx));
    }
    static <T> Step<T> when(Predicate<T> cond, Step<T> step) {
        return (p, ctx) -> cond.test(p) ? step.apply(p, ctx) : Mono.just(p);
    }
}
```

Reusable steps as beans:

```java
@Component @RequiredArgsConstructor
public class Steps {
    private final NationalCodeLookup lookup;
    private final ResilientApiClient api;

    public <T extends HasNationalCode & HasEntries<T>> Step<T> redisLookup() {
        return (p, ctx) -> lookup.findAll(p.nationalCode()).collectList().map(p::withEntries);
    }
    public <T> Step<T> callApi(String name, Function<T, String> uri, BiFunction<T, MessageContext, ?> body) {
        return (p, ctx) -> api.post(name, uri.apply(p), body.apply(p, ctx),
                                    Map.of("Idempotency-Key", ctx.idempotencyKey())).thenReturn(p);
    }
    public <T> Step<T> forward(String topic, Function<T, ?> transform) { /* KafkaSender */ }
}
```

Then a handler is a one-liner composition:

```java
@Component
public class KycUpdateHandler implements TopicHandler<KycUpdate> {
    private final Step<KycUpdate> flow;

    public KycUpdateHandler(Steps s) {
        this.flow = s.<KycUpdate>redisLookup()
                     .then(Step.when(p -> !p.entries().isEmpty(),
                                     s.callApi("kyc-api", p -> "/kyc/refresh", (p, ctx) -> p)))
                     .then(s.forward("kyc.processed", p -> p));
    }

    @Override public String topic() { return "kyc.updates"; }
    @Override public Class<KycUpdate> payloadType() { return KycUpdate.class; }
    @Override public Mono<Void> handle(KycUpdate p, MessageContext ctx) { return flow.apply(p, ctx).then(); }
}
```

Use base classes for the 80 % case and steps for the odd combinations — both satisfy the same `TopicHandler` contract, so the pipeline doesn't care.

